WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:09.000
Classen speaking Ju|'hoan
Recorded in Epukiro, Omaheke Region, Namibia

2
00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:22.000
[Classen introduces himself and his community
in the Ju|'hoan language of the Kalahari]

3
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:38.000
[Ju|'hoan contains 48 click consonants — among the most
complex sound systems ever documented in human language]

4
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:55.000
[The clicks you hear are not decorative — each one
is a distinct consonant that changes meaning entirely]

5
00:00:56.000 --> 00:01:10.000
[The Ju|'hoansi have spoken this language for over
20,000 years across the Kalahari of Namibia and Botswana]

6
00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:25.000
[Their words encode millennia of knowledge —
tracking, medicinal plants, water, stars, and survival]

7
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:40.000
[Approximately 35,000 speakers remain, preserving one of
the oldest living links to humanity's linguistic origins]

8
00:01:41.000 --> 00:02:10.000
[Recorded by Wikitongues volunteer Theron Kolokwe —
part of the global effort to document every language on Earth]
